{"context":"eka-mcp depended on the sibling eka-cli module pinned at v1.0.0, with a local pack.Manifest type workaround — a release-cadence lock: every eka-cli change forced an eka-mcp pin review.\n\nThe plugin contract (Manifest/Artifact/InstallOptions/InstallResult/ContractVersion/Repo/Registry) and the official registry lived in eka-cli/plugin.\n\nADR-023 (eka install command) is obsolete: the subject command was removed (commit 6600f38); its embed mechanism was separately revived by the ADR-020 amendment (init writes the EKA standard declaration via root-level //go:embed).","decision":"Option A2: the plugin contract types and the official registry relocate to eka-core/plugin (the shared library).\n\neka-cli keeps its runner/Discover logic and imports the contract from eka-core/plugin; its local plugin/ package is deleted.\n\neka-mcp drops the eka-cli dependency entirely and removes the local pack.Manifest workaround, importing the contract from eka-core/plugin.\n\nRelease order becomes eka-core → eka-cli → eka-mcp (consumers bump go.mod after the eka-core release).\n\nADR-030 supersedes ADR-023.","consequences":"Single canonical contract source.\n\nConsumer repos decoupled — no sibling imports.\n\nConsumer go.mod bumps required on contract changes.\n\nHistorical snapshot references to eka-cli/plugin remain immutable.\n\neka-mcp version jump eka-core v1.0.0 → current develop pin requires CI verification.","alternativesConsidered":"1. Keep the contract in eka-cli (status quo) — the release-cadence lock persists.\n\n2. Duplicate the types in eka-mcp — drift risk, no canonical source.\n\n3. Extract to a standalone module — heavier governance, no other consumer justifies it yet."}